<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Buganda Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Uganda Agreement (1900), Buganda Agreement (1900), Uganda Agreement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Buganda Agreement was signed on 10 March 1900 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, represented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Sir Harry Johnston</a>, and the regents of the young kabaka Daudi Chwa of Buganda, led by the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apolo-kagwa">Apolo Kagwa</a>. It confirmed the kabaka and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lukiiko">Lukiiko</a> under British protection, fixed the kingdom's boundaries and divided its land, granting large freehold estates (mailo) to chiefs. It made Buganda the privileged core of the Uganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Buganda was the most powerful state of the region when British officials arrived, and they entered Uganda through a centralised kingdom rather than a series of separate societies. Their arrival was complicated by the rivalry of Protestant and Catholic missions and the succession war of 1888-1892, in which Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> of the Imperial British East Africa Company backed the Protestant faction of the Katikiro Apolo Kagwa. The war bankrupted the company, which gave up its control.</p><p>A treaty between Sir Gerald Portal and Kabaka Mwanga on 29 May 1893 informally made Uganda a British protectorate, and on 27 August 1894 Mwanga was compelled to sign a further treaty with Colonel H.E. Colvile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> sought an agreement rather than a treaty so that its rule would rest on law and not merely on fact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The agreement was negotiated by Alfred Tucker, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Uganda, and signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Sir Harry Johnston</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and by Kagwa and other regents for the infant Kabaka Daudi Chwa. Unlike the earlier treaties it set clear boundaries, a system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> and taxes. Buganda became a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> with equal rank among the others, its revenue merged with the protectorate's and its people subject to protectorate law where the agreement made no exception.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> was recognised as native ruler so long as he and his chiefs obeyed that law, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lukiiko">Lukiiko</a>'s role was enhanced and his own reduced, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> gained a veto over future choices of Kabaka. Cases between natives and non-natives went to British courts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Baganda</a> kept no rights to the forests beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> for building and firewood, and the northern boundary was fixed on the River Kafu, giving Buganda lands promised by Colvile in 1894 for its help against Bunyoro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The agreement entrenched the largely Protestant Bakungu chiefs, on whom the few British officials relied to govern and collect taxes. In 1935 Governor Sir Philip Mitchell tried to recast relations by replacing the provincial commissioner with a Resident, but the agreement bound the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> to take advice only on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lukiiko">Lukiiko</a> resolutions, and British influence declined. Its provisions on the Kabaka and Lukiiko were largely reversed by the Buganda Agreement of 1961, and two of the lost counties, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyaga">Buyaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugangaizi">Bugangaizi</a>, returned to Bunyoro after the referendum of 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Buganda Agreement (1900)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Buganda Agreement</b> of 1900, negotiated by Sir Harry Johnston with the regents of the young Kabaka Daudi Chwa II, led by Katikkiro Apolo Kagwa, Britain recognised the kabaka, the Lukiiko and the chiefs, and distributed large estates of freehold mailo land to the chiefs and the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Uganda Protectorate had been built around the Kingdom of Buganda, whose chiefs had helped the British extend their rule under the <b>Uganda Agreement</b> of 1900, and it also included the kingdoms of Bunyoro, Toro and Ankole, the Busoga states and the peoples of the north and east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Buganda Agreement</b> of 1900, negotiated by Harry Johnston, set the terms of British rule and Buganda's privileged place.</blockquote><a href="/state/uganda-protectorate">Uganda Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
